WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.840
Season 1 of "Loki" was a mind-bending trip
to the outer reaches of the MCU.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.950
As always, Marvel packed in plenty of Easter
eggs, from obscure '90s product placement

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:15.160
to a ton of fun references to Marvel Comics
and other movies.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.550
Here are some small details you may have missed.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:23.770
While the Marvel Studios animated logo rolls
at the beginning of Episode 6, we hear Steve

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:27.970
Rogers and Peggy Carter's song "It's Been
a Long, Long Time" play.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:32.200
As the tune begins, several famous lines uttered
throughout the MCU can be heard.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:37.470
It's a mixed bag of more than a dozen catchphrases,
punchlines, and tender moments.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.960
We hear Steve Rogers talking to himself in
"Endgame," which is fitting for a "Loki" episode.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.490
"I can do this all day."

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:44.610
"Yeah, I know."

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.970
Most of the lines we hear are from major MCU
characters, like the Hulk, addressing Loki

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:52.130
after he tosses him around like a ragdoll:

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.370
"Puny God."

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:57.920
But we also hear everyone's favorite Kiwi-accented
rock monster, Korg:

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:58.920
"Hey man.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.380
We're about to jump on that ginormous spaceship.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:02.380
Wanna come?"

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:06.940
And we get one line from a Disney+ MCU TV
show, and of course it's the one the internet

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.020
just couldn't get enough of:

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:13.750
"What is grief, if not love persevering?"

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.400
There's some indistinct overlapping chatter
at the end of this opening sound collage,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.610
but a few other familiar voices can be heard
throughout the introduction as well.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:29.430
As we view the timeline's physical expression,
voiceovers can be heard from several real-life

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:30.430
heroes.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.820
The first comes from Neil Armstrong, saying
"One small step for man..." followed by climate

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.409
activist Greta Thunberg shouting, "How dare
they!"

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:42.920
Activist Malala Yousafzai can be heard saying,
"My dream" followed by Nelson Mandela saying

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.689
"We have fought for the right to experience
peace."

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:50.939
These real-world quotes are wrapped up with
poet Maya Angelou saying, "I will rise."

00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:55.460
In addition to serving as a sweet look back
at the MCU's past, this introduction also

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:59.350
offers a nod to the franchise's rapidly approaching
multiverse.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:04.020
After all, as the MCU begins to embrace the
concept of countless realities, who's to say

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.220
we aren't part of the whole phenomenon?

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:10.549
Slipping a meta brain-bender into the opening
credits — that's so "Loki."

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:15.329
There's a lot to unpack in the first two minutes
of Episode 6.

00:02:15.329 --> 00:02:19.579
After "It's Been a Long, Long Time" fades
out, Tchaikovsky's main score from "Swan Lake"

00:02:19.579 --> 00:02:20.579
fades in.

00:02:20.579 --> 00:02:25.430
The famous ballet centers around Odette and
Odile, two versions of the same character.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:29.909
Odette is a cursed queen who is forced to
live her life as a swan during the day and

00:02:29.909 --> 00:02:31.260
a human at night.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:36.450
Odile is the opposite in all respects, a bewitching
presence who disguises herself as Odette in

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:39.659
order to trick Prince Siegfried into falling
for her.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:42.779
In the ballet, the two parts are often played
by the same dancer.

00:02:42.779 --> 00:02:47.419
Loki and Sylvie's story is much like that
of "Swan Lake," as it's essentially two versions

00:02:47.419 --> 00:02:49.870
of the same character battling one another.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:54.409
While the ballet ends on a rather happy note,
with Odette and Prince Siegfried coming together

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:59.449
and living happily ever after, we don't go
into Episode 6 expecting happy endings for

00:02:59.449 --> 00:03:01.799
Loki or Sylvie anytime soon.

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:07.900
In Episode 5, all signs seemed to be pointing
to the impending appearance of Kang the Conqueror

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.219
in the finale.

00:03:09.219 --> 00:03:14.159
Episode 6 confirms those suspicions, as Kang
appears in the flesh in the Citadel at the

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:15.209
End of Time.

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:20.010
Jonathan Majors was confirmed to play Kang
in the upcoming "Ant-Man and the Wasp: Quantumania"

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:25.939
in 2020, so while his name is never uttered
in "Loki" Episode 6, hardcore MCU fans knew

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:28.689
who he was as soon as he appeared onscreen.

00:03:28.689 --> 00:03:33.480
Miss Minutes, meanwhile, introduces him as
He Who Remains, the TVA string-puller our

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:37.620
heroes have been questing for, and a familiar
name in the comics.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.529
Kang's history in the comics is long and rather
confusing, creating countless possibilities

00:03:42.529 --> 00:03:44.059
for the MCU.

00:03:44.059 --> 00:03:49.359
First appearing in 1964's "The Avengers" #8,
Kang is born Nathaniel Richards and lived

00:03:49.359 --> 00:03:55.939
on Earth-6311, an alternate version of the
main Marvel Comics reality, Earth-616.

00:03:55.939 --> 00:04:00.349
After discovering time travel, he journeys
to ancient Egypt and becomes Pharaoh Rama-Tut,

00:04:00.349 --> 00:04:05.959
but is met with resistance by a time-traveling
Fantastic Four, setting up a long and incredibly

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:10.829
convoluted chain of events that's seen him
battle and ally with numerous Marvel characters

00:04:10.829 --> 00:04:12.790
during the ensuing decades.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:17.580
Kang also has significant ties to Ravonna
in the comics, and with her on the loose as

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:22.019
the "Loki" finale closes, it's hard not to
assume she'll meet up with one of the many

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:28.700
versions of Kang who seem likely to appear
as the MCU's Phase 4 moves forward.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:34.380
The penultimate episode of "Loki" on Disney+
was a wild, Variant-filled ride that set up

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:37.180
what is sure to be a mind-blowing finale.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:40.680
It was also jam-packed with fun Easter eggs
for fans to find.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.290
Here are a few you may have missed in Episode
5, "Journey Into Mystery."

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:51.110
At the beginning of Episode 5, we travel through
a destroyed cityscape full of decrepit buildings.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:54.810
Most of these structures seem like filler
to emphasize the destruction, but there's

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:59.270
one significant Easter egg hidden within the
rubble that's worth noting.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:04.900
Toward the end of the camera pan, a building
on the right reads "Qeng" — "Q-E-N-G" — at

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.110
the top of it.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:10.080
Marvel Comics fans should know this famous
name as it's related to a major villain who's

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.260
set to appear in the MCU.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:16.370
This tower represents Qeng Enterprises, and
if you thought it looked like the Avengers

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:18.370
Tower, you weren't wrong.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:22.830
In the comics, it's revealed that Stark Industries
sold the building to the company in "Avengers"

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:25.380
Vol. 6 #0 from 2015.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:31.690
The CEO of Qeng Industries is Mr. Gryphon,
aka Nathaniel Richards, who exists on Earth-6311

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:37.860
— an alternate version of Earth-616, the
reality where all the main Marvel stuff goes

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:38.860
down.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:43.090
Nathaniel Richards is also known as Kang the
Conqueror, as the latter was split into two

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:46.820
different versions of himself due to a timestream
error.

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:52.100
It's a complicated story from 2015's "All-New,
All-Different Avengers" #1, but this nod to

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:56.930
Kang — a major villain we know will appear
in "Ant-Man and the Wasp: Quantumania" — is

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:59.010
something that we can't ignore.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:04.220
During Episode 5, one of the first things
we see in the Void is a menacing purple-black

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.490
cloud with a monstrous face within it.

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:07.840
"This is The Void.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:08.840
That's Alioth.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:10.810
And we're his lunch.

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:11.810
Come on!'

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:17.180
Known as a trans-temporal entity, Alioth doesn't
exist in any reality, but instead what the

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:19.890
comics describe as Temporal Limbo.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:24.430
The being is the biggest enemy of Kang the
Conqueror, and its empire is bigger than his

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:25.430
foe's.

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:30.310
Alioth first appeared in 1993's "Avengers:
The Terminatrix Objective" #1 and is known

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:34.800
for its absorption powers, which we saw play
a huge role in Episode 5.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.500
Alioth is drawn to living beings more than
anything and has the power [to disintegrate

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:41.620
them as soon as it encompasses them.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:46.591
In the MCU, it appears as if Alioth lives
within the Void at the end of time, unlike

00:06:46.591 --> 00:06:48.330
its location in the comics.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:52.780
It's also being used to protect the person
or entity behind the Time Keepers, who many

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.650
now suspect to be Kang.

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:59.190
If this is the case in the MCU, the two would
be on the same side, a massive shift from

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:00.960
their comic book origins.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:06.880
In a move we never thought would happen, the
Thanos-Copter became canon in the Marvel Cinematic

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:09.810
Universe thanks to "Loki" Episode 5.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:14.200
When the Mad Titan finally perished in "Avengers:
Endgame," it looked like we might never see

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.680
the famous aircraft, but now, we know why
we never did.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:23.780
The Thanos-Copter first appeared in 1979's
"Spidey Super Stories" #39 and was a helicopter

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:28.720
the villain used one time to attack Hellcat,
a popular Marvel hero who fought the Titan

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:31.710
as he tried to obtain the Cosmic Cube.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:37.120
Thanos also picked up Deadpool in the helicopter
in Deadpool Vol. 5 #45, when the latter was

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:39.880
hired to help the Mad Titan steal the cube.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:43.420
It's such a shame we never got to see that
scene play out on the big screen.

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:48.120
Fortunately, "Loki" Episode 5 paid homage
to a great — and frequently mocked — comic

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:49.120
book moment.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:53.600
The Thanos-Copter matched its comic book appearance
exactly, with its bright yellow paint and

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.520
the Titan's name written across it in large
bold letters.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.590
We knew we'd be getting a version of the trickster
god from the classic "Vote Loki" comic after

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:04.380
he appeared in several trailers for the series.

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:08.990
And he finally arrived in Episode 5 but only
for a brief scene where a scuffle breaks out

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:10.970
between a gaggle of Lokis.

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:16.090
As for the variant's origin story, the four-part
comic series debuted in June 2016 and was

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:19.990
a satire on the United States presidential
election of the same year.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:23.460
In the fourth and final issue, Loki concedes
the race.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:27.840
In the comics, Loki's campaign is based on
his honesty about how he's going to lie to

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:28.890
the American public.

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:33.649
The citizens eat it up, except for a reporter
from the Daily Bugle, who's trying her best

00:08:33.649 --> 00:08:34.879
to take him down.

00:08:34.879 --> 00:08:39.620
It's definitely one of the weirdest Loki stories
of all time, complete with "Political Action

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:41.800
Cults" sacrificing goats.

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:45.910
And we're incredibly glad this dapper and
politically ambitious Loki got to show up

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:49.080
on the screen, even if it was only for a few
minutes.

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:53.730
Just before the Loki group makes their way
down to their secret hideout, we see different

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:56.389
layers of rubble above their little base.

00:08:56.389 --> 00:09:01.819
And buried in all this debris, we get a glimpse
of Mjolnir and a small glass jar with "T365"

00:09:01.819 --> 00:09:03.660
written on it.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:08.470
Trapped inside this jar is a tiny version
of Thor ... and one who's a bit more amphibious

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:10.689
than we're used to seeing the God of Thunder.

00:09:10.689 --> 00:09:16.879
Known as Throg, this powerful frog version
of Thor first appeared in "Thor" #363, although

00:09:16.879 --> 00:09:22.040
#365 is more significant for the character
because it's where he lifted Mjolnir and became

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:24.180
the Thunder Frog officially.

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:28.559
This coincides with Mjolnir being just out
of Throg's reach in the episode, as he's trapped

00:09:28.559 --> 00:09:34.769
in the glass jar. "Thor" #365 also heavily
features Loki, who suspects something is afoot

00:09:34.769 --> 00:09:35.809
with his brother.

00:09:35.809 --> 00:09:40.559
It's a hilarious storyline which includes
Thor helping an army of frogs battle an army

00:09:40.559 --> 00:09:41.559
of rats.

00:09:43.559 --> 00:09:48.740
Cooper in Episode 1, Episode 5 of "Loki" further
explained some great American mysteries.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:53.690
The first to appear in "Journey Into Mystery"
was "Polybius" - an arcade game long considered

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:54.999
an urban legend.

00:09:54.999 --> 00:10:00.290
The legend of "Polybius" suggests this fictitious
'80s game was real and run by the government

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:02.379
as a psychological experiment.

00:10:02.379 --> 00:10:08.009
It was supposedly created to test mind control
on users, but the arcade game was never found.

00:10:08.009 --> 00:10:12.149
It looks like it was swooped up by the TVA
and sent to the place where urban legends

00:10:12.149 --> 00:10:13.149
go to die.

00:10:13.149 --> 00:10:18.279
In the middle of "Episode 5," we also see
the USS Eldridge fall out of the sky and into

00:10:18.279 --> 00:10:19.439
the Void.

00:10:19.439 --> 00:10:22.970
Unlike "Polybius," the famous naval ship was
an actual thing.

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:29.339
In 1943, it was alleged that the ship was
made invisible as part of the famous Philadelphia

00:10:29.339 --> 00:10:30.339
Experiment.

00:10:30.339 --> 00:10:35.850
As some claim, it was also apparently teleported
from Philadelphia to Virginia, and back again.

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:40.459
Crew members who were on board reportedly
went insane, and the story was so bizarre

00:10:40.459 --> 00:10:44.999
it was adapted into a time-travel movie — "The
Philadelphia Experiment" — in 1984.

00:10:45.999 --> 00:10:50.959
Since Episode 5 mostly took place in the Void,
set designers had a major opportunity to include

00:10:50.959 --> 00:10:54.119
an incredible amount of Easter eggs from the
MCU's past.

00:10:54.119 --> 00:10:58.749
Some of these include "Ant-Man" villain Yellowjacket's
helmet in the first act, which we saw as we

00:10:58.749 --> 00:11:03.839
panned over the landscape, and Ronin's Dark
Aster spaceship also makes a brief appearance.

00:11:03.839 --> 00:11:07.920
We also get a brief look at a demolished Helicarrier
sitting in the wasteland.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:13.500
Also, in the vast devastation across "Loki"
Episode 5 is what appears to be a stone face

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:18.329
of the Living Tribunal — which we know isn't
an MCU relic... yet.

00:11:18.329 --> 00:11:22.199
One of the most important entities in Marvel
Comics is the character who first appeared

00:11:22.199 --> 00:11:26.029
in "Strange Tales" #157 from 1967.

00:11:26.029 --> 00:11:30.939
The Living Tribunal basically oversees everything,
ever, in every reality.

00:11:30.939 --> 00:11:34.709
Serving as somewhat of a judge of the entire
cosmic universe and all of its realities,

00:11:34.709 --> 00:11:41.029
the Tribunal is more powerful than all of
the Infinity Stones combined.

00:11:41.029 --> 00:11:45.230
Episode 4 of Loki was the most revealing and
game-changing installment yet.

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:50.439
The episode also had some treats in store
for Marvel Comics fans and MCU diehards.

00:11:50.439 --> 00:11:54.139
Here are a few Easter eggs you may have missed
in Loki Episode 4.

00:11:55.139 --> 00:11:59.639
One of the biggest surprises to come in Episode
4 is the return of Jaimie Alexander as Lady

00:11:59.639 --> 00:12:00.639
Sif.

00:12:00.639 --> 00:12:05.209
We hadn't seen the character in the MCU since
2013's "Thor: The Dark World," and while we

00:12:05.209 --> 00:12:10.300
suspected the missing hero might be returning,
her appearance was still shocking nonetheless.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:14.869
Sif's cameo was brief, and those unfamiliar
with Norse mythology might have missed a famous

00:12:14.869 --> 00:12:16.819
story hiding in plain sight.

00:12:16.819 --> 00:12:20.800
When she first encounters Loki, Sif is holding
a lock of her dark hair.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:26.199
She charges at Loki, beyond aggravated that
he cut off her tresses, and calls him "pathetic"

00:12:26.199 --> 00:12:29.509
and "conniving" in addition to other unpleasant
words.

00:12:29.509 --> 00:12:34.399
She aggressively knees and punches him, and
Loki has to relive the event over and over

00:12:34.399 --> 00:12:36.660
as he's stuck in a time loop.

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:40.640
While it might seem like a storyline the "Loki"
writers conjured up, they actually pulled

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:43.269
it directly from the two character's mythologies.

00:12:43.269 --> 00:12:47.959
In the Norse myths, unlike the MCU, Sif is
known for her long golden hair.

00:12:47.959 --> 00:12:53.329
In the myths, Loki cuts off Sif's hair, which
puts Thor — her husband — in a fit of

00:12:53.329 --> 00:12:54.329
rage.

00:12:54.329 --> 00:12:58.320
After his brother charges him with replacing
her hair, Loki travels to Nidavellir — where

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:03.689
Stormbreaker was forged in Infinity War — so
the dwarves can create new hair for Sif out

00:13:03.689 --> 00:13:06.499
of gold which would grow once placed on her
head.

00:13:07.499 --> 00:13:11.619
We finally get a look at the Time-Keepers
in Episode 4 after their appearances were

00:13:11.619 --> 00:13:15.800
teased with TVA sculptures throughout earlier
episodes of the series.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:20.259
When Loki and Sylvie are brought before them
to be pruned, the Time-Keepers give them a

00:13:20.259 --> 00:13:21.589
chance to speak.

00:13:21.589 --> 00:13:25.760
We don't get much out of these mysterious
beings, although their disdain for the Loki

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:27.080
variants is obvious.

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:32.850
With the help of Hunter B-15, who's had her
memories of her pre-TVA life restored by Sylvie,

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:38.529
the Lokis are able to battle and defeat their
captors, and Sylvie severs the head of a Time-Keeper

00:13:38.529 --> 00:13:41.869
— only to discover that they're just androids.

00:13:41.869 --> 00:13:46.339
Beyond disappointed, Loki and Sylvie are left
wondering who created the TVA.

00:13:46.339 --> 00:13:48.459
Marvel Comics might have some answers.

00:13:48.459 --> 00:13:54.689
The Time-Keepers first appeared in 1979's
"Thor" #282 and were created by He Who Remains

00:13:54.689 --> 00:13:57.629
— someone unmentioned during Episode 4.

00:13:57.629 --> 00:14:02.190
Also known as Him Who Remains — or more
simply Him — this mystery being was the

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:07.470
last director of the TVA, who put the Time-Keepers
in place to oversee the organization.

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:14.060
Originally, Him created the Time-Twisters
in 1976's "Thor" #243, another trio of beings

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:17.980
who strayed from their directive and began
destroying Earth every 3000 years.

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:23.489
In "Loki," the time-looping devices used by
the TVA are known as "Time-Twisters," in an

00:14:23.489 --> 00:14:27.439
apparent nod to these characters.

00:14:27.439 --> 00:14:33.509
"Sorry, the Time Twister just loops you, not
the furniture."

00:14:33.509 --> 00:14:38.239
While we don't know who specifically created
the TVA in the world of "Loki," He Who Remains

00:14:38.239 --> 00:14:40.629
— or the Time-Twisters — might have some
insight.

00:14:41.629 --> 00:14:46.070
Different versions of Loki are teased in Episode
2 during a briefing Mobius gives to a group

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:51.199
of Minutemen and other TVA workers before
they take off to Oshkosh, Wisconsin.

00:14:51.199 --> 00:14:55.350
These variants are displayed on a hologram,
which depicts five different versions of the

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:56.350
character.

00:14:56.350 --> 00:15:01.160
None of these guys, however, match the four
new Lokis we meet in the Episode 4 mid-credits

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:02.160
scene.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:06.959
Thanks to the credits, we know these variants
are Boastful Loki, Classic Loki, and Kid Loki.

00:15:06.959 --> 00:15:10.089
Oh yeah: There's also a crocodile Loki!

00:15:10.089 --> 00:15:14.260
Classic Loki sports the traditional garb the
character wore when he first appeared in 1962's

00:15:14.260 --> 00:15:16.649
"Journey Into Mystery" #85.

00:15:16.649 --> 00:15:23.620
Kid Loki has a much shorter history in the
comics, first appearing in 2010's "Thor" #617.

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:28.240
This incarnation came about after the original
Loki died, but he was reborn as a child who

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:30.689
eventually joins the Young Avengers.

00:15:30.689 --> 00:15:35.189
The backgrounds on our other two variants
are vague, but Boastful Loki's possession

00:15:35.189 --> 00:15:38.569
of a hammer could be a nod to 2020's "Thor"
#4.

00:15:38.569 --> 00:15:43.389
In this comic, Thor's brother is able to lift
Mjolnir while on Jotunheim.

00:15:43.389 --> 00:15:48.259
Loki was also able to lift Mjolnir during
Marvel's AXIS event, when his moral alignment

00:15:48.259 --> 00:15:51.889
was reversed, which made him the God of Heroism.

00:15:51.889 --> 00:15:56.589
Comic precedents aside, Boastful Loki's appearance
at least hints at some sort of confirmation

00:15:56.589 --> 00:15:58.860
that a Loki variant is worthy.

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:03.279
As far as the crocodile goes, there seems
to be no history of this character in the

00:16:03.279 --> 00:16:04.279
comics.

00:16:04.279 --> 00:16:08.820
We've seen a frog version of Thor before,
a.k.a. Throg the Frog of Thunder, in 2009's

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:11.489
"Lockjaw and the Pet Avengers" #1.

00:16:11.489 --> 00:16:18.139
And Loki turns Thor into a frog in "Thor"
#363, so hey, why not a crocodile Loki?

00:16:19.139 --> 00:16:23.110
After Loki gets pruned, he winds up in New
York City, where he meets his new variant

00:16:23.110 --> 00:16:24.110
buddies.

00:16:24.110 --> 00:16:27.839
Most of the focus in this scene is on the
new Lokis, and while your eyes are drawn to

00:16:27.839 --> 00:16:32.680
a crocodile wearing gold horns, there's something
major you might have missed in the background.

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:38.069
Avengers Tower — previously known as Stark
Tower — is looming in the distance, absolutely

00:16:38.069 --> 00:16:39.069
destroyed.

00:16:39.069 --> 00:16:42.149
We've seen the building take a beating before,
but nothing like this.

00:16:42.149 --> 00:16:46.570
The new Lokis appear to be hiding out in another
apocalyptic event, although this time, it

00:16:46.570 --> 00:16:50.709
seems to have already happened, as opposed
to previous episodes when the events were

00:16:50.709 --> 00:16:51.709
imminent.

00:16:51.709 --> 00:16:56.809
Avengers Tower is decimated in this timeline,
with its top floor balconies obliterated as

00:16:56.809 --> 00:16:58.259
it rests on a tilt.

00:16:58.259 --> 00:17:03.379
It looks like the Barn Door Protocol wasn't
installed on this former superhero hangout,

00:17:03.379 --> 00:17:07.270
like it was for Avengers HQ in Endgame.

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:11.710
Marvel Studios' "Loki" has introduced a whole
new variant on everyone's favorite god of

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:12.710
mischief.

00:17:12.710 --> 00:17:16.991
With the mysterious Sylvie running around
the timeline with Loki in tow, the MCU is

00:17:16.991 --> 00:17:19.650
getting weirder than it's ever been before.

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:22.839
Here are the Easter eggs you missed in "Loki"
episode 3.

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:29.380
Much of "Loki" Episode 3 is spent on Lamentis-1,
a rocky, purple-skied moon facing imminent

00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:30.380
destruction.

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:34.760
The episode is even named after Lamentis,
which has briefly appeared in Marvel Comics.

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:40.510
Debuting in 2007's "Annihilation: Conquest
Prologue" #1, it lies on the outskirts of

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:41.690
Kree space.

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:46.040
That appearance is basically just a mention,
which means that for all intents and purposes,

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:50.230
Lamentis-1 is basically a blank slate for
the Marvel Cinematic Universe - a perfect

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:54.600
spot for "Loki" to continue delving into its
narrative mysteries, in other words.

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:59.220
Loki and Sylvie arrive on Lamentis-1 after
escaping from Ravonna and her Minutemen following

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:02.690
a skirmish outside the elevator doors leading
to the Time Keepers.

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:09.000
They land on the moon in 2077, 12 hours before
it's set to collide with its planet - an inconvenient

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:13.370
apocalypse for them to hide in, but with their
TemPad low on juice, this odd couple of Lokis

00:18:13.370 --> 00:18:15.870
is forced to make the best of a bad situation.

00:18:15.870 --> 00:18:17.710
"I'm not the only tech-savvy Loki."

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:20.210
"Don't ever call me that."

00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:21.210
"Tech-savvy?"

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:22.210
"No, a Loki."

00:18:23.210 --> 00:18:27.800
After Sylvie and Loki sneak on board the shuttle
ferrying ticket-holders off Lamentis-1, they

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:32.880
have time to start getting to know one another
- so naturally, talk turns to their love lives.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:37.110
Sylvie remains as cagey as ever, but tells
Loki she's managed to keep a long-distance

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:41.580
relationship going with a "postman" as she
runs from one apocalypse to another.

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:45.920
"I've managed to maintain quite a serious
long-distance relationship with a postman

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:48.810
whilst running across time from one apocalypse
to another."

00:18:48.810 --> 00:18:52.880
At first, this sounds like a joke - as if
she could maintain any kind of relationship

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:57.260
given her current life path - but as it turns
out, there's more than one Postman of note

00:18:57.260 --> 00:18:59.900
in Marvel Comics that fans may know about.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:06.110
First appearing in 2002's "Morlocks" #1, Postman
(a.k.a. David) is a mutant who lost control

00:19:06.110 --> 00:19:08.990
of his powers and accidentally wiped his wife's
mind.

00:19:08.990 --> 00:19:13.980
He seeks refuge with the Morlocks based in
Chicago and eventually becomes their leader.

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:18.820
Postman ends up losing his mutant abilities
thanks to the Scarlet Witch, whose "No More

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:23.990
Mutants" decree in 2005's "House of M" #7
wipes the mutant gene from the vast majority

00:19:23.990 --> 00:19:25.160
of its carriers.

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:29.420
Of course, Sylvie and Postman don't appear
to have a clear connection in the comics,

00:19:29.420 --> 00:19:31.850
so we'd say put a pin in this one.

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:35.800
It's not necessarily a hint that the Postman
character is coming, but across the movies

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:40.790
and TV shows of phase 4, the MCU is clearly
setting up something, and it'll be hard to

00:19:40.790 --> 00:19:45.520
know what were real hints and what were just
coincidences until the series lays all its

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:46.800
cards on the table.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:47.800
Multiverse?

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:48.800
Mutants?

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:49.800
Who knows.

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:54.640
Another famous postman is Willie Lumpkin,
the friendly mailman most known for his appearances

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:59.700
in the "Fantastic Four" comic books, and also
for dating May Parker in "The Amazing Spider-Man."

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:00.870
So who knows?

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:02.540
Maybe Sylvie had a thing for him.

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:06.460
There may look like there's a bit of an age
gap, but these are Lokis we're talking about.

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:07.920
Looks can be deceiving.

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:12.650
Or, maybe Sylvie really is just pining for
a hunky delivery man she's never been able

00:20:12.650 --> 00:20:13.690
to settle down with.

00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:18.290
It wouldn't be the first time an Asgardian
super-being has fallen for a regular Joe or

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:19.290
Jane.

00:20:20.290 --> 00:20:25.070
We're treated to some fun callbacks to earlier
MCU chapters in "Loki," and Episode 3 is no

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:26.070
exception.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:29.530
Once Loki and Sylvie work their way onto the
Lamentis shuttle, they sit and converse in

00:20:29.530 --> 00:20:31.070
one of its upper-scale lounges.

00:20:31.070 --> 00:20:36.910
As they talk, Loki helps himself to two glasses
of champagne, and Sylvie ends up falling asleep.

00:20:36.910 --> 00:20:41.170
When she wakes, her new travel companion has
treated himself to more beverages, and he's

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:44.280
putting on a show for the other travelers
in the lounge.

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:49.590
After singing and giving cheers to Sylvie,
Loki smashes a martini glass and yells "Another!"

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:50.810
"Another!"

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:57.080
Loyal MCU fans will notice this nod to the
first "Thor" film - the line and gesture are

00:20:57.080 --> 00:21:02.130
uttered by Thor when he visits a diner with
his new Earth pals Jane Foster, Erik Selvig,

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:03.630
and Darcy Lewis.

00:21:03.630 --> 00:21:07.690
"This drink, I like it.

00:21:07.690 --> 00:21:08.690
Another!"

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:12.570
The line is one of the most quoted from the
original "Thor" movie, which feels so long

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:13.940
ago at this point.

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:17.100
All of these characters have been through
so much at this point.

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:21.650
It's nice that the MCU can still slow down
and show that it remembers where it came from.

00:21:21.650 --> 00:21:27.610
Disney+'s "Loki" is off to a fantastic start,
with the first two episodes positively packed

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:31.970
with fun details and homages for Marvel Comics
fans to find.

00:21:31.970 --> 00:21:34.760
Here are some Easter eggs in Episode 2 you
may have missed.

00:21:36.029 --> 00:21:41.510
At the beginning of Episode 2, several Minutemen
travel to Oshkosh, Wisconsin, in 1985.

00:21:41.510 --> 00:21:45.910
They arrive at a Renaissance Faire where they
believe the Loki variant is hiding out.

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:50.500
At first, it might appear like Oshkosh is
a totally random place for the writers and

00:21:50.500 --> 00:21:55.140
producers of "Loki" to select for a scene
like this, but there's actually a sweet story

00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:56.290
behind it.

00:21:56.290 --> 00:22:01.520
Mark Gruenwald was a prolific Marvel Comics
writer, editor, penciler, and celebrated Marvel

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:06.810
continuity expert, which is why the appearance
of the comic book version of Mobius is based

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:11.950
on Gruenwald, and why Owen Wilson gets to
sport a moustache in the show.

00:22:11.950 --> 00:22:16.350
Mobius's appearance isn't the only homage
"Loki" pays to the late comic book legend,

00:22:16.350 --> 00:22:21.210
as it turns out he was also born in Oshkosh,
Wisconsin, according to his New York Times

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:22.210
obituary.

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:25.880
Gruenwald died at age 43 following a heart
attack in 1996.

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:31.950
There are hundreds of floors that make up
the TVA, which is full of winding hallways

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:34.500
and curvy architectural designs.

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:38.560
Loki and Mobius take several elevator rides
throughout the first few episodes, traveling

00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:41.130
to all different kinds of TVA departments.

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:46.690
In Episode 2, they land on level 372, where
Mobius instructs Loki to read through all

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:51.130
of the variant's paperwork to see if he can
come up with any theories or suggestions.

00:22:51.130 --> 00:22:52.590
Why 372?

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:58.220
The Time Variance Authority made its first
appearance in "Thor" volume 1, issue #372.

00:22:58.220 --> 00:23:03.940
The use of 372 as one of the floor numbers
in "Loki" is a nice nod to the organization's

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:09.170
origins and suggests viewers should probably
take a second look at all numbers that pop

00:23:09.170 --> 00:23:10.170
up in the series.

00:23:11.270 --> 00:23:16.460
Loki can only access his own file at the TVA,
so we get to witness Loki read about the events

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:20.390
of Ragnarok, which he hasn't lived through
at this point in time.

00:23:20.390 --> 00:23:24.070
While he's looking through the documents,
we see some new tidbits of information about

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:26.600
the event that we witnessed in "Thor: Ragnarok."

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:32.460
The paperwork shows that there were 9,719
casualties in what's described as a Class

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:34.150
Seven Apocalypse.

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:39.090
Mobius confirms that Ragnarok wiped out "most"
of Asgard's people, and we know from "Avengers:

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:43.470
Endgame" that the survivors are currently
living on Earth in New Asgard.

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:48.140
A more light-hearted easter egg hidden in
the Ragnarok paperwork is the mention of the

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:49.140
Revengers.

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:53.420
The makeshift superhero group is assembled
by Thor and includes Valkyrie , the Hulk,

00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:54.420
and Loki.

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:59.620
The foursome bond together to escape Sakaar
and are instrumental in the takedown of Hela.

00:23:59.620 --> 00:24:04.180
Thor jokes about their group name in the film,
but the group helped save hundreds of Asgardians

00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:06.650
from death — justifying their mention on
the document.

00:24:07.730 --> 00:24:13.280
In Episode 2, Loki, Mobius, and a team of
Minutemen travel to Alabama in 2050.

00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:18.460
They're there to sniff out the missing variant
who Loki suspects is hiding in apocalyptic

00:24:18.460 --> 00:24:19.460
events.

00:24:19.460 --> 00:24:23.460
The group arrives in a Roxxcart parking lot,
and the store name is too reminiscent of a

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:26.130
famous Marvel Comics corporation to be ignored.

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:30.710
The Roxxon Corporation — which has gone
by many names — is an oil conglomerate that

00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:35.650
was first mentioned in 1974's "Captain America"
#180.

00:24:35.650 --> 00:24:37.880
Clearly this is the same company now.

00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:42.890
Roxxcart has worked with both SHIELD and HYDRA,
but their alliance will always lie with whoever

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:44.630
can fill their pockets the most.

00:24:44.630 --> 00:24:49.870
Blinded by dollar signs, Roxxcart deals in
heavily illicit activities, sometimes with

00:24:49.870 --> 00:24:51.740
the universe's biggest villains.

00:24:51.740 --> 00:24:57.360
In "Thor" #6 Vol. 4, Roxxon worked closely
with Malekith to receive exclusive rights

00:24:57.360 --> 00:24:59.510
to mine on any planets he overtook.

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:04.100
In the MCU, the corporation was mentioned
by the Mandarin in "Iron Man 3."

00:25:04.100 --> 00:25:05.100
-

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:08.140
"Thomas here is an accountant for the Roxxon
Oil Corporation."

00:25:08.140 --> 00:25:13.260
And the brand has been featured in other Marvel
live-action TV projects, including Agents

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:14.260
of S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:25:15.260 --> 00:25:19.290
There’s been a long standing rumor that
Lady Loki would make an appearance in Loki,

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:25.260
so when a female antagonist popped up toward
the end of Episode 2, a casual fan and rumor-follower

00:25:25.260 --> 00:25:28.410
might just assume that we’ve got ourselves
a Lady Loki!

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:30.750
Well… not so fast my friend.

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:36.130
In the English credits at the end of the episode,
actress Sophia Di Martino is listed as playing

00:25:36.130 --> 00:25:37.130
“The Variant”.

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:42.180
However, if you look at the Spanish credits,
Elisa Beuter is credited as playing Sylvie.

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:44.980
That name might ring a bell to long-time Marvel
fans.

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:50.212
It seems likely this Variant is actually Enchantress
- a character who first battled Thor way back

00:25:50.212 --> 00:25:54.040
in 1964 in Journey Into Mystery issue 103.

00:25:54.040 --> 00:25:56.170
Enchantress’s real name is indeed Sylvie.

00:25:56.170 --> 00:26:01.800
Now, we’ve seen the MCU get cute before
with cameos and such - looking at you Wandavision

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:05.150
- so we can’t guarantee you that this is
Enchantress.

00:26:05.150 --> 00:26:09.770
Lady Loki still might pop up eventually in
the series, or this is another of those silly

00:26:09.770 --> 00:26:12.610
MCU twists Enchantress is the new Lady Loki.

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:19.320
After The Variant sets off multiple reset
charges at Roxxcart in 2050, the sacred timeline

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:21.240
is thrust into chaos.

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:25.610
As Judge Renslayer watches the pandemonium
in her office, her computer screen displays

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:29.710
several locations that appear to be affected
by all of the meddling.

00:26:29.710 --> 00:26:33.780
While some of these locations we've seen on
more than one occasion in the MCU — like

00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:37.390
Asgard and Vormir — the others we only visited
once.

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:42.300
Hala is the Kree home planet and the one that
Carol Danvers trained on for many years, and

00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:43.690
it appeared in "Captain Marvel."

00:26:43.690 --> 00:26:47.800
Xandar made its appearance in "Guardians of
the Galaxy," but we know the planet was decimated

00:26:47.800 --> 00:26:51.510
by Thanos just before the events of "Avengers:
Infinity War."

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:56.400
In "Thor: Ragnarok," we visited the trash
planet of Sakaar, which the Grandmaster oversees,

00:26:56.400 --> 00:27:01.020
while Titan is the home planet of Thanos that
saw an epic battle between the villain and

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:03.220
part of the Avengers in "Infinity War."

00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:07.880
Ego is, of course, the living planet, and
the setting for about half of "Guardians of

00:27:07.880 --> 00:27:10.800
the Galaxy Vol. 2."

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:17.120
"Loki" is the third Marvel series to land
on Disney+, and the premiere is just as strange,

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:20.650
if not more, than the frequently mind-bending
"WandaVision."

00:27:20.650 --> 00:27:25.460
And, like "WandaVision," there are some fun
Easter eggs tucked away for Marvel fans to

00:27:25.460 --> 00:27:26.460
find.

00:27:27.460 --> 00:27:30.690
When Episode 1 of "Loki" kicks off, a brief
recap of the events leading up to the series

00:27:30.690 --> 00:27:31.870
plays out.

00:27:31.870 --> 00:27:36.360
After Loki is captured at the finale of "The
Avengers," we see him escorted by the superhero

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:39.260
team to the bottom floor of Avengers tower.

00:27:39.260 --> 00:27:44.520
As we saw in "Avengers: Endgame," a disgruntled
Hulk causes a future Tony Stark to drop a

00:27:44.520 --> 00:27:48.500
briefcase containing the Tesseract, which
then bounces in front of Loki's feet.

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:53.680
The trickster grabs the cube and vanishes,
disrupting the entire time heist.

00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:58.390
We find out that this portal's other end was
in the Gobi Desert in Mongolia, where Loki

00:27:58.390 --> 00:28:00.040
drops out of the sky.

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:04.070
His head sticks out of the sand as he lays
on his back, the desert's landscape partly

00:28:04.070 --> 00:28:06.180
covering his green and gold garb.

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:10.820
While it's a brief moment in the episode's
intro, it's also a major callback to the MCU

00:28:10.820 --> 00:28:13.310
property that started it all: "Iron Man."

00:28:13.310 --> 00:28:17.890
When Tony escapes from his captors in that
film, he flies off into the sky, but he comes

00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:22.700
crashing down into the desert sand after his
home-brewed suit malfunctions, breaking apart

00:28:22.700 --> 00:28:26.200
into hundreds of pieces and leaving him dazed
on the ground.

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:30.540
The shot with Loki is a nice nod to the first
"Iron Man" and a pleasant parallel that depicts

00:28:30.540 --> 00:28:33.530
the aftermath of two men who have just escaped
capture.

00:28:34.530 --> 00:28:37.830
After Loki is captured by the TVA, he's taken
to their headquarters.

00:28:37.830 --> 00:28:42.780
He signs some paperwork, walks through a temporal
aura detector, and then takes a number as

00:28:42.780 --> 00:28:45.830
he waits to see a judge for his crimes against
the timeline.

00:28:45.830 --> 00:28:50.350
While waiting, he's treated to an educational
video produced by the TVA to explain to variants

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:51.960
why they've been captured.

00:28:51.960 --> 00:28:56.670
Hosted by TVA mascot Miss Minutes, the video
goes into detail about the creation of the

00:28:56.670 --> 00:29:00.030
TVA and how the Time Keepers monitor the timestream.

00:29:00.030 --> 00:29:04.340
Miss Minutes then explains that when variants
veer off their predetermined course.

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:10.920
"Stepping off your path created a Nexus event,
which left unchecked, could branch off into

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:14.760
madness, leading to another multiversal war."

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:18.809
This isn't the first time we've heard the
word "nexus" in the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:29:18.809 --> 00:29:23.760
— and the last time wasn't long before "Loki":
One of the six "WandaVision" commercials seen

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:28.530
during the series' show-within-a-show is an
advertisement for Nexus antidepressants.

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:33.950
"Nexus: Because the world doesn't revolve
around you.

00:29:33.950 --> 00:29:35.450
Or does it?"

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:40.410
Nexus will be a significant word and concept
moving forward in the MCU, as evidenced by

00:29:40.410 --> 00:29:44.800
the title of the "Doctor Strange" sequel,
"Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness,"

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:47.690
which is being written by "Loki" head writer
Michael Waldron.

00:29:47.690 --> 00:29:52.400
In "WandaVision," the Nexus drug was being
advertised as a medicine for Wanda Maximoff's

00:29:52.400 --> 00:29:56.650
grief and anger — and also a reference to
her role as a Nexus Being.

00:29:56.650 --> 00:30:00.710
"Loki" is poised to further explore what the
term means in the MCU.

00:30:01.710 --> 00:30:06.320
When Loki is apprehended in the Gobi Desert,
several TVA officers arrive through a portal

00:30:06.320 --> 00:30:07.490
to capture him.

00:30:07.490 --> 00:30:11.380
While it's not specifically stated who they
are or what their title is, these guards seem

00:30:11.380 --> 00:30:16.350
to be a nod to Marvel Comics' Minutemen based
on their purpose and uniforms.

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:21.930
First appearing in "Fantastic Four" #352,
the Minutemen are charged with policing the

00:30:21.930 --> 00:30:22.930
timestream.

00:30:22.930 --> 00:30:26.650
As these TVA guards serve the same sort of
purpose, their appearance is a nice Easter

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:27.650
egg.

00:30:27.650 --> 00:30:31.300
In the Marvel comics, the Minutemen are actually
robots, not human beings as they appear to

00:30:31.300 --> 00:30:32.300
be in "Loki."

00:30:32.300 --> 00:30:37.270
However, it should be noted that at the beginning
of Episode 1, a TVA worker asks Loki to clarify

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:39.730
that he is not a "fully robotic being."

00:30:39.730 --> 00:30:43.690
"Do a lot of people not know if they're robots?"

00:30:43.690 --> 00:30:47.850
It's probably nothing more than a throwaway
gag, but Marvel is nothing if not tricky.

00:30:47.850 --> 00:30:48.850
Who knows?

00:30:48.850 --> 00:30:52.330
The guards — or Minutemen — could actually
be artificial.

00:30:53.330 --> 00:30:57.110
Sure, Owen Wilson's Agent Mobius is a bit
of a time cop, but that's not why he's sporting

00:30:57.110 --> 00:30:58.120
that moustache.

00:30:58.120 --> 00:31:03.430
In the comics, Agent Mobius' look was based
on the late Mark Gruenwald, a prolific Marvel

00:31:03.430 --> 00:31:08.140
comics writer and editor known for being Marvel's
resident continuity expert until his untimely

00:31:08.140 --> 00:31:10.870
death at age 43 from a heart attack.

00:31:10.870 --> 00:31:15.420
So it makes sense that the live-action Mobius
would keep the 'stache from the comics, and

00:31:15.420 --> 00:31:18.690
bring this sweet homage to a Marvel legend
into the MCU.

00:31:18.690 --> 00:31:21.350
